\title[Moonshine from the Null Cone]{Moonshine from the Null Cone:\\
the Berggren Groupoid, Even Lorentzian Lattices,\\ and the Holy Construction}

\author{Aristotle}

\date{14 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The tree of primitive Pythagorean triples generated by the three Barning--Hall matrices
lives on the integer null cone of the Lorentzian lattice
$\Lor=(\ZZ^3,\,aa'+bb'-cc')$.  The even unimodular Lorentzian lattice $\mathrm{II}(25,1)$,
whose null cone carries Conway's holy construction of the Leech lattice and whose
arithmetic underlies monstrous moonshine, is another integral Lorentzian geometry.  We
determine, exactly, the relationship between the two.  We prove: (A) a parity obstruction
--- no map whatsoever, linear or not, from $\Lor$ into an even lattice preserves the
bilinear form, because $e_1=(1,0,0)$ has odd norm $1$, so the Pythagorean null cone does
not embed isometrically in $\mathrm{II}(25,1)$; (B) a positive repair --- after rescaling
the form by $2$, the Barning--Hall monoid embeds \emph{faithfully}, by root reflections,
into the isometry group of every even lattice carrying an orthogonal frame of norms
$(2,2,-2)$, the mechanism being an explicit factorisation of the three generators into
reflections in five unit-norm vectors; (C) two refutations of the combinatorial and
arithmetic forms of the correspondence --- the ternary tree's level and ball cardinalities
never equal $23$ or $24$ and every finite labelling has an infinite fibre, while the
\emph{Gaussian-split law} (no prime $p\equiv 3 \bmod 4$ divides a node hypotenuse)
excludes the entire moonshine head data $196883$, $196884$, $21296876$, $21493760$ and the
constants $23$, $24$, the prime spectrum being exactly $\{p \text{ prime}: p\equiv 1
\bmod 4\}$; and (D) a rigidity theorem --- at \emph{every} primitive isotropic vector of
$\Lor$ the Conway quotient $\rho^{\perp}/\ZZ\rho$ is the rank-one unimodular lattice
$\langle 1\rangle$, i.e.\ $A_1$ after doubling, never the rootless Leech lattice.  The
unifying moral: rootlessness is a strictly even phenomenon, and parity is the single
invariant separating elementary Pythagorean geometry from the exceptional geometry of
moonshine.
\end{abstract}

